# Renderer comparison and shader-cache investigation — 31 October 2026

The supplied guidance separates loading-screen presentation, total time to play and first real driving/effect use. These need independent measurements. This pass does not establish an Android renderer winner or a complete fix for first-use shader stalls.

## Desktop renderer observations

The pinned Godot 4.7.2 Windows build ran the actual-App Star fixture with two private profiles, Compatibility first and Mobile/Vulkan second. Both reported the intended actual renderer, 1280×720, 2× MSAA and desktop scale 1. Both completed eight readiness/pause assertions. First/repeat elapsed times were 59.75/2.50 s for Compatibility and 38.96/3.50 s for Mobile; largest observed callback intervals were 7.83 s/145 ms and 5.01 s/132 ms respectively.

These are **not a controlled speed comparison**. Production files changed between runs, exact starting per-file manifests were not retained, and global driver caches and shared-host load were uncontrolled. Callback intervals are not actual presented-frame gaps. Both offline runs emitted a Windows certificate-store error. The captured Mobile images visibly differ from Compatibility, including a broad warm haze and stronger dark backing near HUD elements. A post-processing/depth path and translucent HUD drawing are hypotheses only; no ablation established a cause. Captures, logs, comparison code and detailed limits are in [desktop renderer evidence](evidence/renderer-comparison-2026-10-31/README.md). No renderer setting was changed in the shipping project.

Godot's [pipeline documentation](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/pipeline_compilations.html) describes precompilation and ubershaders for Mobile/Forward+ but not Compatibility. This supports testing Mobile on target devices; it does not guarantee faster loading or equivalent visuals.

## GLES3 cache extraction

The pinned [GLES3 shader implementation](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/ed1daf0bf/drivers/gles3/shader_gles3.cpp) can write compiled program binaries to its cache. A passive, bounded interceptor in an owned Windows Compatibility App process observed 445 binary extractions after initialization. Of these, 256 were second-or-later extractions of the same observed program-handle lifetime, requesting 46.8 MB and consuming 30.619 ms of cumulative CPU call time. All extraction calls together consumed 50.743 ms; only about 8.37 ms overlapped the 7.729-second worst observed App callback interval. Filesystem writes, compile/link calls and first-draw GPU work were not measured by this trace; the remainder is unattributed in this run.

This confirms repeated extraction, not the total cost or achievable benefit of cache-write coalescing. Deferring saves also needs bounded storage, context ownership, invalidation on version changes/free, failure recovery, atomic replacement of a valid cache and a new-process reload test. Disabling the whole cache would change reads as well as writes and is not a coalescing A/B. The raw bounded trace, diagnostic source, observations and runner false-negative caveat are in [cache evidence](evidence/shader-cache-observation-2026-10-31/README.md). No engine patch or unsupported hook is enabled in the game.

## Android comparison preparation

Initial read-only inspection found one authorized OPD2403 device with the owner's game foreground. The owner subsequently explicitly authorized use of the tablet and interruption of that session. Two task-owned packages were then installed and launched, using separate package IDs, a common copied source snapshot and private records. The production app data was not cleared. A host collector under `tools/android_renderer_comparison/` polls SurfaceFlinger during loading and driving and keeps raw observations. Its offline simulation passes; missing boundaries, unsupported layers and poll gaps must remain explicit. A probe-side start gate permits attaching the collector before the App workload. Logged touch receipt is not physical input-to-photon latency.

The common private preview bake passed the six-image, 139-source provenance check. The first private import was stopped after five minutes; read-only copies of workspace import and Gradle cache data seeded retries, so this was not a clean import. An initial audit found five newly baked previews with stale imported checksums. Later private imports completed, and each independent audit matched all 1,130 in-scope imported source/output checksums. Quick startup, driving, catalog and menu checks passed for each variant. An initial release export failed because conflicting task-only Android preference variables prevented Gradle's location service from starting. The corrected private offline environment passed its Gradle check, and both release APKs subsequently built; their signatures, package IDs and absence of INTERNET permission were verified. The logs retain an ICU/text-server warning.

The strict packaged-byte comparison **failed**. Four generated scenes differ in node IDs, and sparse-pack/DEX-related generated entries also differ. Follow-up byte and Godot `SceneState` investigations explain those differences without establishing Android runtime equivalence. Independently generated per-car preview revisions and full build IDs also differ, and affect cache keys. Direct inspection verifies that the identical Compact header template fails its revision guard for both variants; its byte-identical packaged template has SHA-256 `f5ed4354cc15048cbf50a0b10b6774d6ecf8ebef2b817eedb0a1feef70f39fde`. Other cache paths and runtime cache hits remain unmeasured. The artifacts are therefore diagnostic builds, not a proven matched performance pair. Detailed logs, SHA-256 values, manifest/scene comparisons and limitations are recorded under `build/android-renderer-comparison-20261031/` and in the [harness README](../tools/android_renderer_comparison/README.md).

Driver caches may be shared between test packages; neither package-specific fresh data nor a fresh process proves a cold driver. Android still needs a matched comparison of actual renderer selection, presented loading frames, ready time, first real effect use, memory, appearance and lifecycle. A single Adreno device cannot establish Mali or older-device coverage.

## Exploratory tablet runs

The test device was OPD2403, Android SDK 35, Adreno 750, with a reported 50 Hz display. The two offline release apps reported the intended `gl_compatibility` and `mobile` renderer; the Mobile device log reported Vulkan 1.3.128. The runs were sequential, with differing cache and thermal histories and unverified runtime cache parity. Source inspection also shows Compatibility on mobile Adreno may use an FXAA path while Mobile does not; the probe did not record effective AA. These are **not matched-quality A/B results**.

| Probe observation | Compatibility | Mobile/Vulkan |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Completed cases | 17/17, no reported workload errors | 15/17; first Star race unavailable and incomplete case set reported |
| First Star loading phase | 36.634 s; 2.342 s maximum observed App callback interval | The App's 45 s deadline expired at “Loading track”; no completion measurement |
| Subsequent Star loading phase | 8.224 s; 145.8 ms maximum App callback interval | 35.909 s; 2.725 s maximum, after the failed first attempt |
| App startup | 4.117 s, zero frame samples | 5.311 s, zero frame samples |

These numbers are App callback observations, not physical frame presentation or shader-call timings. Zero startup samples are missing data, not proof of smoothness. The Mobile timeout is the App's own deadline, not evidence of an OS kill; its cause is not established. The automatic driver can trigger a false-start penalty: the later warm instrumented screenshots show false-start banners, while primary run launch status was not recorded. The driving workload therefore does not prove identical trajectories or complete effect coverage.

The Compatibility first Star load reported 397 cooperative car-build yields over 7.97 s; the later Mobile Star attempt, after the failed first load, reported 1,153 yields over 23.12 s. Each yield waits for another process frame; at a reported 50 Hz the ratios are approximately 20 ms per yield, but the runs do not establish how much time was idle or why counts differed. The configured 4 ms work budget is soft: the recorded largest individual slices were about 56/67 ms. A small desktop headless 4/8 ms budget experiment retained exact geometry but was strongly affected by run order and cannot establish Android responsiveness or a safe new budget. No budget was changed.

SurfaceFlinger polls retained valid actual-present timestamps, but phase markers were collected without device timestamps, the ring can overwrite entries, and phase-boundary coverage was not established. Long observed gaps cannot be assigned confidently to a loading phase. A separate warmed, perturbed screenshot/memory/thermal pass captured unsynchronized views; it showed visual differences but cannot establish pixel parity. Its single driving PSS samples were 527,209 KiB Compatibility and 1,226,914 KiB Mobile; these are not peak or GPU-memory measurements, and differing state/order/AA prevents attributing the difference to renderer choice. [Device findings](evidence/android-renderer-trial-2026-10-31/DEVICE_FINDINGS.md), [analysis](evidence/android-renderer-trial-2026-10-31/pair-analysis.json), [raw observations](evidence/android-renderer-trial-2026-10-31/raw-observations.zip), and the [Compatibility](evidence/android-renderer-trial-2026-10-31/compatibility-drive.png) and [Mobile](evidence/android-renderer-trial-2026-10-31/mobile-drive.png) screenshots retain the observations.

A subsequent, separate cache-warm Compatibility Perfetto run observed a 4.867 s App startup callback interval and a 4.830 s gap between valid SurfaceView ring timestamps whose endpoints appeared in three polls. The correlation is suggestive but OEM timestamp interpretation, invalid/pending rows and approximate phase boundaries limit a hardware-presentation claim. Critically, Perfetto recorded **zero native CPU samples**, and its scheduler buffer lost data; the cause of missing samples is unproven. No GPU render-stage data or individual shader-call attribution was obtained. [Trace evidence](evidence/android-perfetto-observation-2026-10-31/README.md) describes its scope, clock checks and retained raw trace. After the captures, only the two task-owned probe packages were uninstalled; the [cleanup receipt](evidence/android-renderer-trial-2026-10-31/device-cleanup-receipt.json) records their removal and the production package remains installed.

A separate hash-verified tablet trace of a task-owned shell process recorded 244 sample rows, all without usable call stacks and all reported skipped. It does not explain the missing samples in the game trace. For future builds, the probe now waits until after the countdown and early-throttle penalty window to assign its automated driver, and records its takeover and launch status. Offline builder checks and a pinned Godot launch-rule test pass; the revised probe has not been rebuilt or validated on Android, and the recorded runs above are unchanged. See the [harness README](../tools/android_renderer_comparison/README.md).
